\viewkind4\pard\cf1\fs32\b Separate TCP connectivity from service health\b0\fs22\par\par
\pard\sa120 A TCP connection test answers a narrow question: could a handshake complete with this address and port? It does not prove that TLS, authentication or the application protocol will succeed.\par
\pard\li240\ri240\sa120\f1 Example target: 192.0.2.10, TCP port 443\par
Connected: the TCP handshake succeeded.\par
Refused: the connection was explicitly rejected.\par
Timeout: no successful completion within the deadline.\f0\par
\pard\sa120 Refusal can come from a host or network policy; it is not a full application diagnosis. A timeout cannot by itself distinguish filtering, loss, an unreachable path or a silent destination.\par
\pard\sa120 If the input is a hostname, show the resolved address and DNS duration separately from TCP connection duration. IPv4 and IPv6 are separate attempts. Do not silently change destination while presenting the run as one measurement.\par
\pard\sa120 The proposed test sends no arbitrary application data after connecting. Continue to HTTP/TLS inspection when that is the service being investigated.\par
\pard\sa120 \cf2\ul Microsoft \u8212? TCP connection semantics\ul0\cf1  (online: \cf2\ul https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winsock2/nf-winsock2-connect\ul0\cf1 )\par
